A||||9||Homicidal||10" Subwoofer box placement suggestions????||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||05-08-2000||12:58 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.members.home.net/silksteel/Mvc-011s.jpg"> <P>That's the box, measures 17" L 17" W 7 1/4" H.<P>I got it behind the driver's seat (I'm not too big) and it was facing the seat first.  It felt good to me, but the passenger felt nothing.  Plus, the bass right on my back and nowhere else got a big overpowering.<P>So I turned it around to face the firewall.  It feels MUCH better and the bass carries through the car nicer.  But I noticed when I tip the box slightly away from the firewall and left it some space, the bass was more apparent and louder.  Of course it just tips right back again when I put the seat back.<P>Does anybody have any suggestions to increase my bass or maybe a new spot?||||reg||5
Z||000001||FIEROCIOUS||05-08-2000||01:08 AM||||Pop a hole in the side faceing the passenger side! Buy a bass tube from radio shack.<BR>whalla better bass. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Homicidal||05-08-2000||02:52 AM||||How big does the tube have to be?<P>How long, and where should it point?<P>And is this useful in a sealed box?  Aren't tubes more for bandpass?||||reg||1
Z||000003||FIEROCIOUS||05-08-2000||02:00 PM||||When you have the speaker against the wall or seat of your fiero you are basicly makeing a band pass, the sound waves have no were to go. <BR>By porting the box the sound waves are given a place to go. Try a 2inch diameter 4inch long tube. If you can't find a tube at a stereo shop you can make them from ABS pipe from a hardware store. <BR>The tube size realy depends on the box size I'm makeing an educated guess on the size.<BR>Any qualified stereo shop can tell you the correct size. 4" long is as long as I would go with your box. I'm less sure about the diameter, experiment with the 2" first then go larger if needed.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Homicidal||05-08-2000||03:20 PM||||Sounds logical, but where would I place the tube so it wouldn't look so.......dorky?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jay||05-08-2000||09:25 PM||||I've got a new suggestion, build a new box for the passenger side.  I've seen it done under the dash on the left hand side beside the fuel tank bulk head.  The box will fit a 10 or 12 inch sub.  Try JL, they make big bass subs for small enclosures that really pound.  The box is triangular in shape to fit the space up by the passengers feet and still gives lots of leg room.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Homicidal||05-08-2000||10:53 PM||||Actually, I just found a huge discovery.  I had the woofer cone almost FLAT against the firewall.  The waves weren't moving.  So when playing it, I tilted it slightly away from it and let it breathe.  WOW, what a difference!  So I made a "spacer" and stuck it in there.  Now my bass waves are all over!  <BR>Wow, all 1/2" really changed the entire sound dynamics!  It's like when you put your hand over a bandpass box's hole.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||stillofknight||05-08-2000||11:47 PM|||| If you want to port the box, go to the manufacturer's web site. They should have a technical section. You might be ablt to find the port dimensions there. Or see if they have an email address for tech questions. Tell them the dimensions of your box, and they should be able to tell you if it can be ported or not,and if so, what size to make the port.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Homicidal||05-09-2000||12:47 AM||||I can build a ported one.  But it's slightly bigger.  I can also build a bandpass which I plan to do which will use the whole front trunk.  But for now, it's all good!||||reg||1
Z||000009||phillip||05-09-2000||01:52 AM||||mine is set up like jay suggested, except i'm using an 8" jl audio.||||reg||1
